<bp:ProteinReference rdf:about="http://identifiers.org/uniprot/Q92871">
 <bp:entityFeature rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ModificationFeature_e31f6adc2d6c3720a9eddaa03da2c7ea" />
 <bp:entityFeature rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ModificationFeature_05cebd059e75a626c2a1d0dd136eab47" />
 <bp:standardName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Phosphomannomutase 1</bp:standardName>
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/RelationshipXref_hgnc_symbol_PMM1_identity" />
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/UnificationXref_uniprot_knowledgebase_Q92871" />
 <bp:organism rdf:resource="http://identifiers.org/taxonomy/9606" />
 <bp:displayName rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PMM1_HUMAN</bp:displayName>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">5.4.2.8</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PMMH-22</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PMM1</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PMM 1</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PMMH22</bp:name>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://identifiers.org/refseq/NP_002667</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ProteinReference_uniprotkb_Q92871_identity</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PMM1_HUMAN Reviewed; 262 AA.</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://biocyc.org/biopax/biopax-level3ProteinReference102905</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">REPLACED http://pathwaycommons.org/pc12/ProteinReference_HPRD_03473_identity</bp:comment>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FUNCTION: Involved in the synthesis of the GDP-mannose anddolichol-phosphate-mannose required for a number of criticalmannosyl transfer reactions. In addition, may be responsible forthe degradation of glucose-1,6-bisphosphate in ischemic brain.{ECO:0000269|PubMed:16540464}.CATALYTIC ACTIVITY:Reaction=alpha-D-mannose 1-phosphate = D-mannose 6-phosphate;Xref=Rhea:RHEA:11140, ChEBI:CHEBI:58409, ChEBI:CHEBI:58735;EC=5.4.2.8;COFACTOR:Name=Mg(2+); Xref=ChEBI:CHEBI:18420;Evidence={ECO:0000269|PubMed:16540464};ACTIVITY REGULATION: IMP, a metabolite whose concentration iselevated in anoxia, inhibits phosphomannomutase andphosphoglucomutase activities and strongly enhances glucose-1,6-bisphosphatase activity. {ECO:0000250}.BIOPHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES:Kinetic parameters:KM=54 uM for alpha-D-mannose 1-phosphate{ECO:0000269|PubMed:16540464};KM=7.5 uM for alpha-D-glucose 1-phosphate{ECO:0000269|PubMed:16540464};PATHWAY: Nucleotide-sugar biosynthesis; GDP-alpha-D-mannosebiosynthesis; alpha-D-mannose 1-phosphate from D-fructose 6-phosphate: step 2/2.SUBUNIT: Homodimer. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:16540464}.SUBCELLULAR LOCATION: Cytoplasm.TISSUE SPECIFICITY: Strong expression in liver, heart, brain, andpancreas; lower expression in skeletal muscle.SIMILARITY: Belongs to the eukaryotic PMM family. {ECO:0000305}. COPYRIGHT: UniProt Consortium (www.uniprot.org). Distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivs License.</bp:comment>
</bp:ProteinReference>

